body:
> Writing it like this may mislead the reader into thinking that it's "nearest keyframe in terms of distance" and thinking that the answers should be something like this instead:

For example, if you write the intended behavior for each action at the beginning of the test case, such misunderstandings would not occur.

```
// Nearest maintains the keyframe that appears earlier in time until the next key,
// regardless of whether it is Forward or Backward.
// Because of this, Backward becomes a simple reverse playback of Forward.
```

```
// Discrete Update sets the key that was actually passed only once at the exact moment it is passed.
// Unlike Nearest, this means that in the case of Backward,
// each key interval in the Forward animation is offset by one index.
// Simply put, in an animation with a duration of 1s, if there is a key A at 0s and a key B at 1s,
// the Discrete Backward setting will result in the value of key B being visibly displayed for one second.
```

Regarding the t-value of the comment, I assume it indicates the position on the timeline. However, to be honest, it looks like the supplementary note on "Nearest" has become a memo that only you can understand.

